WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.820
Conservation starts with what drove a population down.

2
00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:10.240
If habitat loss is the cause, address it first.

3
00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:16.300
Protect habitat, connect isolated patches, and help damaged ecosystems recover.

4
00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:21.120
Small populations can also need extra help with chance and inbreeding.

5
00:00:22.120 --> 00:00:25.240
A genetic gain may be temporary if habitat keeps shrinking.

6
00:00:26.333 --> 00:00:27.653
What does the evidence show?

7
00:00:28.533 --> 00:00:33.913
In a randomized experiment at one South Carolina site, connected patches of equal

8
00:00:33.913 --> 00:00:37.573
area gained plant species faster and lost them more slowly.

9
00:00:38.333 --> 00:00:41.053
That does not establish how corridors work everywhere.

10
00:00:41.933 --> 00:00:47.513
Across conservation trials, action improved biodiversity or slowed its decline in

11
00:00:47.513 --> 00:00:50.093
about two-thirds of cases compared with no action.

12
00:00:51.373 --> 00:00:52.533
Recovery can still be incomplete.

13
00:00:52.533 --> 00:00:58.393
Active restoration did not, on average, outperform ending the disturbance, though

14
00:00:58.393 --> 00:01:00.013
few studies compared them directly.

15
00:01:01.167 --> 00:01:03.647
Why give small populations extra help?

16
00:01:04.587 --> 00:01:08.207
Chance in who survives and breeds matters more when numbers are low.

17
00:01:09.327 --> 00:01:10.787
Drift erodes genetic variation.

18
00:01:11.827 --> 00:01:13.687
Inbreeding lowers survival and fertility.

19
00:01:14.707 --> 00:01:18.407
Fewer animals mean more inbreeding, which can mean fewer animals again.

20
00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:21.400
The head count also misses something.

21
00:01:22.660 --> 00:01:27.160
Effective size is the size of an idealised population that would lose genetic

22
00:01:27.160 --> 00:01:28.800
variation at the same rate.

23
00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:35.180
Unequal offspring numbers, uneven sex ratios and population swings usually make it

24
00:01:35.180 --> 00:01:36.180
smaller than the count.

25
00:01:37.133 --> 00:01:42.153
Genetic rescue can improve fitness by bringing in animals from another population.

26
00:01:43.113 --> 00:01:48.493
After 8 Texas females were released into Florida, panther numbers about tripled and

27
00:01:48.493 --> 00:01:49.713
heterozygosity doubled.

28
00:01:50.533 --> 00:01:54.573
But managers must weigh disease and whether donors suit local conditions.

29
00:01:55.853 --> 00:01:58.033
Monitor survival, reproduction and genetics.

30
00:01:58.793 --> 00:02:04.553
A later study found the population stabilised and declined from 2016 to 2020.

31
00:02:05.683 --> 00:02:09.983
Population viability analysis helps compare management options.

32
00:02:10.763 --> 00:02:12.723
Run a population model many times.

33
00:02:13.423 --> 00:02:17.443
The fraction that goes extinct within a chosen time estimates the risk.

34
00:02:18.223 --> 00:02:23.923
A minimum viable population is the smallest isolated population meeting a chosen

35
00:02:23.923 --> 00:02:26.043
persistence target in a given habitat.

36
00:02:27.103 --> 00:02:30.003
Read model results with their assumptions and uncertainty.

37
00:02:30.003 --> 00:02:33.903
Even the ranking of options can change with model structure.

38
00:02:35.017 --> 00:02:37.757
Evidence says what an option is likely to do.

39
00:02:38.477 --> 00:02:40.657
It does not choose which outcome matters most.

40
00:02:41.577 --> 00:02:46.517
Set out biological, social and economic goals with the people affected, then compare

41
00:02:46.517 --> 00:02:47.077
the tradeoffs.
